Article 33
Regional Government in preventing the occurrence of acts of violence against women:
a. conducts the preparation and socialization regarding matters related to violence against women;
b. conducts socialization on the rights of Women;
c. endeavors to improve education for Women;
d. opens skills courses for Women to support the economic needs of the family;
e. creates employment opportunities for Women;
f. provides counseling related to Violence Against Women;
g. empowers social community organizations and Women's organizations at the Regional, sub-district, and village levels to increase the knowledge and insight of Women;
h. establishes complaint posts for the Protection of Women; and
i. forms a network in efforts to prevent Violence Against Women.

Article 6
Empowerment of women in the economic field is implemented through:
a. provision of skills and job training;
b. facilitation of the formation of productive economic business groups;
c. facilitation of strengthening and development of productive economic business groups;
d. facilitation and capital assistance; and
e. facilitation of marketing network development.

Article 18
(1) The implementation of protection for homeless women is intended to provide protection and prevention against forms of violence caused by the absence of shelter or permanent residence.
(2) Homeless women as referred to in paragraph (1) shall receive the right to protection for:
a. decent housing;
b. health and social security;
c. population administration registration;
d. opportunities to obtain skills to improve economic status; and
e. access to information and legal consultation services.

Article 26
(1) The Regional Government has the obligation and responsibility for the empowerment and protection of women.
(2) The efforts for empowerment and protection of women as referred to in paragraph (1), include:

a. determination of policies, programs, and activities;
b. determination of implementation guidelines;
c. provision of services; and
d. coordination of policies, programs and activities.

(3) The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in Article 25 letter a include:

a. implementing policies for the Protection of Women from all forms of discrimination and acts of violence;
b. determining policies, programs, and activities for the Empowerment and Protection of Women that have been established;
c. collecting data and information about Women Victims of Violence and Discrimination as well as supporting laws and regulations;
d. conducting education on matters related to the Protection of Women Victims of Violence and Discrimination;
e. conducting socialization of laws and regulations related to the Protection of Women Victims of Violence and Discrimination;
f. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of Protection of Women Victims of Violence and Discrimination.

(4) To anticipate the occurrence of acts of Violence, the Regional Government is obligated to provide and organize services for Victims in the form of:

a. establishing and facilitating the operation of integrated service institutions for Victims by involving community elements;
b. facilitating legal aid assistance and legal services according to the needs of Victims;
c. providing safe houses or alternative residences along with handling mechanisms, psychosocial and spiritual services;
d. conducting continuous handling up to the rehabilitation and social reintegration stage;
e. conducting monitoring and evaluation of the implementation of protection of Victims; and
f. encouraging public awareness of the importance of Protection for Victims.

(5) In carrying out the obligations as referred to in paragraph (3) and paragraph (4), the Regional Government must pay attention to the rights and obligations of Parents, guardians, husbands or other persons who are legally responsible for the Victim.
(6) To carry out the obligations and responsibilities as referred to in paragraph (1) through paragraph (4), the Regional Government shall prepare a regional action plan for the Protection of Women Victims of Violence.
(7) Provisions regarding the regional action plan for the Protection of Women Victims of Violence as referred to in paragraph (6) shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 35
(1) In providing protection services as referred to in Article 34 paragraph (1), the Regional Government:
a. provides services and handling as quickly as possible to witnesses and/or victims;
b. provides convenience, comfort, safety, and free of charge for witnesses and/or victims;
c. maintains confidentiality of witnesses and/or victims;
d. guarantees justice and legal certainty for witnesses and/or victims;
e. provides apparatus, health workers, social workers, and spiritual counselors;
f. creates and develops a system and mechanism for cooperation of service programs involving parties that are easily accessible to victims;
g. provides protection for companions, witnesses, families, and friends of victims;
h. provides decent work and livelihood in accordance with the type and degree of disability, education, and ability; and
i. provides protection for female workers or laborers in accordance with the provisions of protection for laborers or workers in accordance with statutory provisions.
(2) The Regional Government in providing protection services as referred to in paragraph (1) may cooperate with the central government, provincial government, other district/city governments, the community, and/or other social institutions.
(3) Cooperation as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be set forth in a cooperation agreement.

Article 14
Implementation of protection for women is provided to: a. women workers/laborers;
b. elderly women;
c. women with disabilities;
d. homeless women;
e. domestic women workers;
f. women as family economic supporters;
g. women former inmates of correctional institutions;
h. women victims of disasters;
i. women commercial sex workers; and
j. women victims of violence.

Article 28
(1) The Regent implements policies, programs, and activities for the Protection of Women to eliminate all forms of discrimination and acts of Violence Against Women.
(2) Regional Apparatus with authority over Women implements policies, programs, and activities for the Protection of Women that have been established and their implementation is carried out in cooperation and coordination with relevant Regional Apparatus, law enforcement, community institutions, universities, and the community.
(3) The implementation of policies, programs, and activities for the Protection of Women as referred to in paragraph (2) consists of:
a. policy analysis;
b. advocacy;
c. coordination and communication;
d. socialization;
e. education;
f. training;
g. facilitation;
h. provision of services; and
i. other forms.

Article 4
Every female victim of violence has the right: a. to have her dignity and worth as a human being respected; b. health rehabilitation;
c. to determine her own decisions;
d. to obtain information;
e. to confidentiality;
f. to compensation;
g. to social rehabilitation;
h. to complaint handling;
i. to obtain facilitation in the judicial process; and j. to assistance.

CHAPTER III
WOMEN'S EMPOWERMENT
Article 5
Women's empowerment is directed towards obtaining opportunities and rights as human beings in order to be able to play a role and participate in the fields of:
a. economy;
b. social culture;
c. politics and government;
d. law;
e. education;
f. health;
g. employment; and
h. social security.

Article 15
(1) Implementation of protection for female workers/laborers to provide protection from acts of economic exploitation and cruel, inhumane treatment and to pursue proper appreciation for work.
(2) Female workers/laborers as referred to in paragraph (1) receive protection for:
a. recognition of rights, wages and decent working conditions;
b. health insurance and social security for employment;
c. access to information and legal consultation services;
d. opportunities to obtain knowledge and skills to improve status.

Article 24
(1) Forms of violence against Women include:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. domestic neglect;
e. trafficking of women; and
f. exploitation.
(2) Implementers of protection for women victims of violence in order to provide legal protection and/or health recovery.
(3) Protection for women victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (2) includes:
a. legal assistance;
b. assistance following the occurrence of acts of violence;
c. rehabilitation and social reintegration; and
d. health services.

REGENT OF PANGANDARAN
WEST JAVA PROVINCE
REGIONAL REGULATION OF PANGANDARAN REGENCY
NUMBER ... YEAR 2019
CONCERNING
EMPOWERMENT AND PROTECTION OF WOMEN
BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY
REGENT OF PANGANDARAN,
Considering : a. that in order to improve the position, role and quality of women is an effort to realize gender equality and justice in family life, society, nation and state;
b. that all forms of violence against women constitute a violation of human rights so that women need to be protected in their dignity and honor and guaranteed their right to life in accordance with their nature and innate nature without discrimination and empowered so that they can actualize their potential optimally;
c. that to protect the community and realize equity and justice, as an obligation of the regional government to provide protection and welfare for women, a legal foundation is needed in the implementation of empowerment and protection of women;
d. that based on the considerations as referred to in letter a, letter b and letter c, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning Empowerment and Protection of Women.
In View of : 1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 7 of 1984 concerning Ratification of the Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination Against Women (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1984 Number 29, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3277);
3. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
4. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number
4419);
5. Law Number 11 of 2009 concerning Social Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2009 Number 12, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4976);
6. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number
5679);
7. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning Implementation and Cooperation for Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
8. Government Regulation Number 9 of 2008 concerning Procedures and Mechanisms for Integrated Services for Witnesses and/or Victims of Criminal Acts of Human Trafficking (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Year 2008 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number
4818);
With Mutual Approval

Article 29
(1) The Regent in implementing policies, programs, and activities for the protection of women as referred to in Article 28 shall undertake efforts to:
a. coordinate implementation among Regional Apparatus;
b. cooperate with the central government, provincial government, and other regency/city governments in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
c. strengthen the capacity of gender institutions;
d. facilitate services for the Protection of Women;
e. provide services for the Protection of Women;
f. implement affirmative action for the Protection of Women; and
g. compile a data system for the Protection of Women including a data system for Violence Against Women.
(2) The implementation of policies, programs, and activities as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be coordinated by the Vice Regent.

Article 34
(1) Protection services to women are carried out in the form of:
a. protection of the victim's personal security from physical and mental threats;
b. health services according to medical needs;
c. medical services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;
d. maintaining the confidentiality of the victim's identity;
e. providing testimony during court hearings without facing the suspect;
f. spiritual guidance services; and/or
g. protection of Women who work as laborers or workers in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
(2) Protection of the victim's personal security from physical and mental threats as referred to in paragraph (1) letter a is carried out by the family, law enforcement officials, social institutions, and other parties either temporarily or based on a court-issued Protection order.

